WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Safer stack and heap habits begin with reviewable lifetime and ownership.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:11.400
Keep stack lifetimes simple, and do not let pointers, references, iterators, or views escape the objects they describe.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.980
When heap allocation is needed, make the owner, transfer point, and cleanup rule visible in the interface.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.340
Prefer RAII, containers, smart pointers, and bounded views.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.600
Let destructors release resources.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:35.740
Preserve size information with data, validate indexes and externally influenced sizes, and avoid raw buffer assumptions that require every caller to remember hidden rules.

7
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:45.940
Be skeptical of dangling access, stale aliases, invalidated iterators, moved-from objects, and long-lived views into unstable storage.

8
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:55.560
Respect resource limits by avoiding large local objects by default, bounding recursion where needed, and budgeting heap use for variable-size input.

9
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.360
Finally, use tools and workflow to reinforce the design.

10
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:07.460
Sanitizers, static analysis, compiler warnings, boundary tests, regression tests, and focused code review all help catch mistakes earlier.

11
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:15.480
Modernize incrementally, starting with code that handles untrusted input, complex ownership, or manual memory management.

12
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.600
The lasting goal is not to choose stack or heap as a slogan.

13
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.500
It is to make every memory decision understandable.

14
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:33.320
If another engineer can identify lifetime, owner, valid range, cleanup path, and resource limit from the interface and nearby code, the design is far easier to maintain safely.
